Q: How can I use contentChildren on a directive on ng-template? A: <p>No this is not the correct reason.<br />
<code>TestparentDirective</code> is a structural directive and is a content child of your <code>TestComponent</code>.<br />
It itself is never rendered since all it does is carry structure in form of a <code>TemplateRef</code>.<br />
The template it carries is what eventually gets rendered via <code>NgTemplateOutlet</code> in <code>TestComponent</code>.</p>
<p>You are doing it mostly right but your imports are a bit messy.<br />
You only need to import exactly the components whose selector is <strong>directly</strong> used in the component's template.<br />
So you don't need to import components that are rendered via a <code>TemplateRef</code> or are stored in a typescript variable.</p>
<p>The reason it doesn't work however, is most likely a missing import in your App component.<br />
With correct imports it works just fine:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>@Directive({
  selector: '[app-testparent]',
  standalone: true,
})
export class TestparentDirective {
  children = contentChildren(TestchildComponent);

  template = inject(TemplateRef);
  constructor() {}
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-testchild',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [],
  template: '&lt;p&gt;testchild works! data: {{data}}&lt;/p&gt;',
})
export class TestchildComponent {
  @Input() data!: number;
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-test',
  standalone: true,
  imports: [NgTemplateOutlet],
  template: `
    &lt;ng-container *ngTemplateOutlet=&quot;testParent()?.template ?? null; context: {$implicit: 1}&quot;&gt;&lt;/ng-container&gt;
    &lt;ng-container *ngTemplateOutlet=&quot;testParent()?.template ?? null; context: {$implicit: 2}&quot;&gt;&lt;/ng-container&gt;
  `,
})
export class TestComponent {
  testParent = contentChild(TestparentDirective);
}

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [TestComponent, TestchildComponent, TestparentDirective],
  template: `
  &lt;app-test&gt;
    &lt;ng-template app-testparent let-data&gt;
      asdf
      &lt;app-testchild [data]=&quot;data&quot;&gt;&lt;/app-testchild&gt;
    &lt;/ng-template&gt;
  &lt;/app-test&gt;
  `,
})
export class App {}
</code></pre>

Q: The argument type &#39;Future&lt;Widget&gt;&#39; can&#39;t be assigned to the parameter type &#39;Widget&#39;.dart A: <p>Your method must return <code>Future&lt;Widget&gt;</code>, as I'm understanding your question's title is your error, its mean that you returning a <code>Widget</code> not <code>Future&lt;Widget&gt;</code>. To do that, you should wrap your widget inside the Future like this:</p>

<pre><code>Future&lt;Widget&gt; getWidgetAsync(double size) async {
PdfImage val = await pdfImageFromImageProvider(
  pdf: pdf.document,
  image: fw.AssetImage('assets/' + iconPath),
);

return Future.value(Container(
  width: size,
  height: size,
  decoration: BoxDecoration(
    color: layoutbg,
    shape: BoxShape.circle,
  ),
  child: Image(val),
));
</code></pre>

<p>Hope this will work.</p>
